# Monitor overhead measurement

Applies to `monitor/measurement/**` (harness, sampler, FWQ, report) and the
`prepare_rpmbuild_dirs.sh --measure-overhead` path.

## Laws

1. **Headline numbers come only from the release RPM under its real unit properties.** The
   harness installs the `rpmbuild -ba` release RPM and runs it as `hpcperfstats.service`
   (Nice, IOSchedulingClass, sandbox unchanged). An instrumented build (debug, metric
   profiler) is never a headline number; label it and keep it out of `HEADLINE_METRICS`.
2. **Bracketed, replicated blocks.** OFF → NONE → ALL → SOLO(driver groups, seeded shuffle) →
   ALL → OFF, the closing OFF shared with the next block. SOLO deltas are paired with the
   **same block's** NONE leg. Report the interaction residual
   `ALL − [NONE + Σ(SOLO − NONE)]` with its CI and the drift check across blocks. Do not
   replace this with peak-%CPU subtraction or leave-one-out legs.
3. **Collector sets come only from the runtime drop-in**
   `/run/systemd/system/hpcperfstats.service.d/90-measure.conf`
   (`HPCPERFSTATS_DISABLE_TYPES`). Never edit the site conf to disable collectors; preflight
   refuses a site conf with `disable_types` / non-default `collection_profile` and a
   sysconfig that sets those variables. Verify each leg against its own `Enabled types:`
   journal line.
4. **Partial payloads never reach the production queue.** NONE / SOLO / CADENCE legs point
   `ExecStart` at a `/run` copy of the site conf whose only change is
   `queue <queue>_measure` (CADENCE also changes `sample_freq`). Do **not** use `-q` /
   `--sample-frequency` for this: the daemon reads the conf after the CLI, so conf keys win
   (`test_daemon_reads_conf_after_cli_so_queue_needs_a_conf_copy`). Cleanup deletes only
   `<production>_measure` and prints the `rabbitmqctl` command when it cannot.
5. **`NOT_MEASURED`, never 0 or an estimate.** Every metric slot is
   `{value, unit, ci95, n, source}` or `{status: NOT_MEASURED, reason}`. Missing perf, RAPL,
   PSI, broker socket, OFF legs, or an interrupted run each have a named reason.
6. **Provenance is mandatory**: host/CPU/microcode/NUMA/kernel, RPM NVRA + sha256, binary
   sha256, git commit + dirty flag, build capabilities, redacted conf, unit properties, tool
   versions, harness and schema versions. Every reported number must be recomputable from
   `raw/` (`bin/summarize_profile.py`).
7. **A cadence sweep measures; it does not change defaults** (`monitor-jitter-and-fidelity-priority.mdc`).
8. **Driver groups, not types** (`arm_cpu`, `dcgm_gpu` and `hwmon_power` are separate groups).
   A type in an `Enabled types:` line with no group in
   `lib/driver_map.py` fails preflight; `test_driver_map_covers_all_st_names` fails when a new
   `.st_name` lands unmapped. Add the type to the map in the same change as the collector.
   A type that only works with another group's backend goes **in that group**: on Grace
   overlay builds (`configure.likwid_overlay`) `arm_aarch64_imc` is in `arm_cpu`, because alone
   it only waits for the LIKWID overlay (c122-018 2026-11-05: its SOLO leg logged `waiting for
   the LIKWID overlay group` and measured ~0, not the SCF cost). A type with its own fds gets
   its own group (`host_power_socket` → `hwmon_power`). Build-info `cpu_backend` `dcgm` (RPMs
   before the `arm` rename) resolves like `arm`, so a baseline RPM and a candidate get the same
   groups.
9. **Restore on every exit path**: drop-in removed, measure queue deleted, site conf and
   service active/enabled state restored; `restore.sh` is written before the first change.
   The measured package stays installed (reserved node).
10. **Cost a feature inside a group with a VARIANT leg, never a SOLO subtraction across groups.**
   `driver_map.VARIANTS` runs the base group with one measurement-only daemon knob in the same
   runtime drop-in (`arm_cpu_noscf`: `host_cpu_hw` with `HPCPERFSTATS_LIKWID_GRACE_SCF=0`), is
   shuffled with the SOLO legs, and is paired with **SOLO(base) in the same block**
   (`collector_matrix.variants`; excluded from the interaction residual). The pair counts only
   when the leg journal contains the variant's `marker` (the daemon's log that the knob took
   effect) — otherwise `NOT_MEASURED`, so a daemon without the knob cannot report a false 0.
11. **Emitted keys are captured, not assumed.** After every leg on `<queue>_measure` the harness
   drains that queue (management API, guarded to `_measure` only, after the sampler stops) and
   keeps the last `$` schema message as `raw/legs/<id>/schema.txt`; `profile.json`
   `schema_keys` holds keys per type (`NOT_MEASURED` when nothing was captured) and
   `bin/compare_schema_keys.py BASE CAND [--type nvidia_gpu]` exits 1 when a key was removed.
12. **Every profile names its governor and runs per governor** (D13, D25). The same daemon used
   ~8 core-s/h under `performance` and ~109 under `ondemand` at the same ~620 wakeups/s
   (c122-018, finding 18). The default `--governors performance` runs one leg set with every
   CPU pinned to `performance` (user decision 2026-11-06: no low-MHz `ondemand` re-sampling by
   default). A comma list (`performance,dynamic`) runs one full leg set per governor;
   `dynamic` = `ondemand` on `cppc_cpufreq` / `acpi-cpufreq` / passive
   `amd-pstate`, `powersave` on `intel_pstate` / `amd-pstate-epp` (`lib/cpufreq.py`). The
   harness writes `scaling_governor` for every CPU before each set and restores every CPU's
   original governor on every exit path (and in `restore.sh`); a governor the driver does not
   offer fails preflight before any change. Each leg records governor, `scaling_cur_freq`
   min/p50/max and node busy cores. Never quote core-s/h without its governor; never compare
   core-s/h across governors.
13. **Wakeups/s is the headline metric** — voluntary + nonvoluntary context switches of
   **every** daemon thread per second (per-tid `status`; `/proc/<pid>/status` is the leader
   only). It does not depend on the governor, so it is the cross-architecture number;
   `report.md` puts it next to core-s/h per governor. Old raw without per-thread counters
   falls back to the leader and is labelled `main_thread`; new per-thread columns are `null`,
   never 0.
14. **Refuse a busy node.** Preflight measures node busy cores minus the daemon's CPU (the
   OFF-state load) and refuses above 1 core unless `--allow-busy-node` (flagged `busy_node`).
   Round 1 ran with ~17 busy cores in every leg, OFF included (finding 13).
15. **A/B a tool on the live PID before blaming it for a CPU gap.** perf, the 1 Hz sampler and
   the build were each suspected and each ruled out by an A/B on the live daemon before the
   governor was found (findings 10, 12, 18). Compare RPMs only as two harness runs on the same
   node with the same `--governors` (D24), never against an older harness run.
16. **Campaigns compare per leg: never shorten legs, one generated conf everywhere.** Every leg
   of every node uses the same `--leg-seconds` (1800), settle, FWQ, `--blocks` and governor;
   total time, leg count and collector set may differ by architecture. The harness refuses an
   over-budget plan (raise `--max-hours`); `--allow-fit` is never used for a campaign
   (2026-11-09: c122-018's legs were auto-cut to 1200 s while Vista ran 1800 s). Each node gets
   a harness-built `--conf`, identical except `server`/`queue`/`user`/`password`
   (`buffer_hours 6`, `sample_freq 30`, `sample_freq_slow 600`, `send_freq 300`,
   `enable_slow_tier 1`, no `jobid_file`); never a site conf (buffer 48 h / 4096 / 6 h and
   different `jobid_file` made the 2026-11-09 runs incomparable). Each cluster keeps its own
   fleet build; that difference is intended and recorded.
17. **Every Grace node has bound CoreSight PMUs before a run.** Preflight refuses an
   `arm-cs-arch-pmu.*` device without a `driver` link (c642-041 2026-11-09: firmware APMT gave
   `No cpu associated with the PMU`, no `nvidia_scf_pmu_*`, so `arm_aarch64_imc` disabled
   itself). `--allow-unbound-pmu` only for diagnosis, flagged `unbound_pmu`; such a node is
   not part of a campaign.
18. **A campaign is published only with a passing `bin/compare_runs.py`.** Run it over all the
   campaign's run directories; exit 0 (`COMPARABLE`: every must-match row identical, no
   `fit_steps`) is required, and its `compare.md` is committed with the profiles. Intended
   differences (fleet build, OS, hardware, collector groups) are listed, never failures.

## Tests

`.venv/bin/python3 -m pytest -p no:django -q HPCPerfStats/monitor/measurement/tests` and
`monitor/measurement/bin/run_local_checks.sh`; prepare flag:
`monitor/tests/test_prepare_rpmbuild_measure_flag.sh` (registered in `tests/Makefile.am`).
Statistics use fixed seeds; parsers use fixtures captured from a real `/proc`.

## Overlap

- **`monitor-jitter-and-fidelity-priority.mdc`** — cadence is not changed to cut cost.
- **`monitor-systemd-service-contract.mdc`** — measured unit is the shipped unit; the drop-in lives under `/run` only.
- **`monitor-version-and-packaging.mdc`** — spec `build-info/` stash, `Release:` bumps.
- **`monitor-workspace-contract.mdc`** — listend consumes only configured queues; `_measure` is never ingested.
- **`test-runs-output-directory.mdc`** — trial-run logs under `test_runs/`; committed profiles under `docs/measurements/`.
